{"seed":{"mim":"617153","entry_type":"phenotype","prefix":"#","title":"DEVELOPMENTAL AND EPILEPTIC ENCEPHALOPATHY 45","alt_titles":["EPILEPTIC ENCEPHALOPATHY, EARLY INFANTILE, 45"],"symbol":"DEE45","gene_symbols":[],"seed_pmids":[],"max_year":2019},"nodes":[{"id":"entry","type":"Entry","label":"DEVELOPMENTAL AND EPILEPTIC ENCEPHALOPATHY 45","mim":"617153","entry_type":"phenotype"},{"id":"section:description","type":"Section","label":"description"},{"id":"section:nomenclature","type":"Section","label":"nomenclature"},{"id":"section:cloning_expression","type":"Section","label":"cloning_expression"},{"id":"section:gene_structure","type":"Section","label":"gene_structure"},{"id":"section:clinical_features","type":"Section","label":"clinical_features"},{"id":"section:other_features","type":"Section","label":"other_features"},{"id":"section:biochemical_features","type":"Section","label":"biochemical_features"},{"id":"section:inheritance","type":"Section","label":"inheritance"},{"id":"section:cytogenetics","type":"Section","label":"cytogenetics"},{"id":"section:mapping","type":"Section","label":"mapping"},{"id":"section:heterogeneity","type":"Section","label":"heterogeneity"},{"id":"section:gene_function","type":"Section","label":"gene_function"},{"id":"section:molecular_genetics","type":"Section","label":"molecular_genetics"},{"id":"section:genotype_phenotype_correlations","type":"Section","label":"genotype_phenotype_correlations"},{"id":"section:pathogenesis","type":"Section","label":"pathogenesis"},{"id":"section:diagnosis","type":"Section","label":"diagnosis"},{"id":"section:clinical_management","type":"Section","label":"clinical_management"},{"id":"section:population_genetics","type":"Section","label":"population_genetics"},{"id":"section:evolution","type":"Section","label":"evolution"},{"id":"section:animal_model","type":"Section","label":"animal_model"},{"id":"section:history","type":"Section","label":"history"},{"id":"paper:31618474","type":"Paper","label":"Burgess et al. (2019)","pmid":"31618474","year":2019,"content_level":"fulltext","relevance":"supporting","title":"The Genetic Landscape of Epilepsy of Infancy with Migrating Focal Seizures."},{"id":"paper:27273810","type":"Paper","label":"Lien et al. (2016)","pmid":"27273810","year":2016,"content_level":"title","relevance":"primary","title":"A second patient with a De Novo GABRB1 mutation and epileptic encephalopathy."},{"id":"paper:26950270","type":"Paper","label":"Janve et al. (2016)","pmid":"26950270","year":2016,"content_level":"fulltext","relevance":"supporting","title":"Epileptic encephalopathy de novo GABRB mutations impair γ-aminobutyric acid type A receptor function."},{"id":"paper:23934111","type":"Paper","label":"Epi4K Consortium et al. (2013)","pmid":"23934111","year":2013,"content_level":"fulltext","relevance":"primary","title":"De novo mutations in epileptic encephalopathies."},{"id":"finding:23934111:1","type":"Finding","label":"Epi4K Consortium et al. (2013) sequenced the exomes of 264 trios: 149 probands with infantile spasms and 115 with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, plus their parents. They found a de novo mutation in GABRB1 in 1 proband. They noted that 6 de novo mutations hit subunits of the GABA ionotropic receptor (4 in GABRB3 and 1 each in GABRA1 and GABRB1). They suggested that rare mutations in different genes may converge on shared pathways.","pmid":"23934111","section":"molecular_genetics"},{"id":"gene:GABRB1","type":"Gene","label":"GABRB1"},{"id":"variant:GABRB1:raw:de novo mutation in gabrb1","type":"Variant","label":"GABRB1 de novo mutation in GABRB1","gene":"GABRB1","keys":["raw:de novo mutation in gabrb1"],"omim_name":"GABRB1"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0012469","type":"Phenotype","label":"Infantile spasms","hpo_id":"HP:0012469"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0002197","type":"Phenotype","label":"Generalized-onset seizure","hpo_id":"HP:0002197"},{"id":"inh:AD","type":"Inheritance","label":"Autosomal dominant","hpo_id":"HP:0000006"},{"id":"finding:23934111:2","type":"Finding","label":"In the Epi4K Consortium et al. (2013) cohort, patients with infantile spasms were required to show hypsarrhythmia on EEG. Patients with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome were required to show EEG background slowing and generalized spike-and-wave or generalized paroxysmal fast activity. All patients were required to have no moderate-to-severe developmental impairment before seizure onset. Their MRI or CT had to be normal or show only mild diffuse atrophy or focal cortical dysplasia.","pmid":"23934111","section":"clinical_features"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0002521","type":"Phenotype","label":"Hypsarrhythmia","hpo_id":"HP:0002521"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0010850","type":"Phenotype","label":"EEG with spike-wave complexes","hpo_id":"HP:0010850"},{"id":"finding:23934111:3","type":"Finding","label":"Epi4K Consortium et al. (2013) found significant genetic heterogeneity underlying infantile spasms and Lennox-Gastaut syndrome. De novo mutations were enriched in genes intolerant to functional variation (P = 2.9 x 10(-3)). GABRB3 (4 patients) and ALG13 (2 patients) showed significant evidence of association with the disorder.","pmid":"23934111","section":"heterogeneity"},{"id":"gene:GABRB3","type":"Gene","label":"GABRB3"},{"id":"gene:ALG13","type":"Gene","label":"ALG13"},{"id":"finding:26950270:1","type":"Finding","label":"Janve et al. (2016) studied a de novo heterozygous F246S mutation in the GABRB1 gene, which the Epi4K Consortium had found in a child with infantile spasms. F246 is a residue that is the same in all GABA-A receptor subunits and lies in transmembrane domain 1. When expressed with alpha-1 and gamma-2L subunits in HEK293T cells, the mutation reduced GABA-evoked peak current density by 25% in the homozygous condition and had no effect in the heterozygous condition. It slowed current deactivation. The authors concluded that the mutation impaired GABA-A receptor function and suggested that GABRB1 is a candidate human epilepsy gene.","pmid":"26950270","section":"molecular_genetics"},{"id":"variant:GABRB1:p.F246S","type":"Variant","label":"GABRB1 p.F246S","gene":"GABRB1","keys":["p.F246S"],"omim_name":"GABRB1, PHE246SER"},{"id":"finding:26950270:2","type":"Finding","label":"In single-channel recordings, Janve et al. (2016) found that receptors with the GABRB1 F246S mutation had normal open probability despite a lower opening frequency. The mutation produced a gain of function, with about 3.75-fold longer mean open time and about 3.2-fold longer burst duration. It also lowered single-channel conductance to about 21 pS. The authors proposed that faster rise time and slower deactivation would lengthen inhibitory postsynaptic currents but would reduce charge transfer during later currents, resulting in a net loss of GABAergic inhibition.","pmid":"26950270","section":"pathogenesis"},{"id":"finding:26950270:3","type":"Finding","label":"Janve et al. (2016) found that the GABRB1 F246S mutation raised whole-cell holding currents about 5-fold in the homozygous condition, and these currents were partly blocked by zinc. In the absence of GABA, it increased spontaneous single-channel open probability 4-fold. Surface levels of the mutant beta-1 subunit were slightly but significantly lower, while total levels were unchanged. The authors suggested that receptors containing the mutant subunit opened spontaneously. Structural modeling predicted rearrangements in the coupling zone that spread to the transmembrane domains.","pmid":"26950270","section":"pathogenesis"},{"id":"finding:27273810:1","type":"Finding","label":"Lien et al. (2016) reported a second patient with epileptic encephalopathy who carried a de novo mutation in the GABRB1 gene.","pmid":"27273810","section":"molecular_genetics"},{"id":"variant:GABRB1:raw:de novo gabrb1 mutation","type":"Variant","label":"GABRB1 De Novo GABRB1 mutation","gene":"GABRB1","keys":["raw:de novo gabrb1 mutation"],"omim_name":"GABRB1"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0200134","type":"Phenotype","label":"Epileptic encephalopathy","hpo_id":"HP:0200134"},{"id":"finding:31618474:1","type":"Finding","label":"In a patient (Patient 63) with epilepsy of infancy with migrating focal seizures (EIMFS), Burgess et al. (2019) used whole-exome sequencing to identify a de novo heterozygous variant in GABRB1 (chr4:47405633T>C, GRCh37), predicted to cause an ile247-to-thr (I247T) substitution. The variant was not found in the ExAC or gnomAD databases. SIFT predicted it to be deleterious, PolyPhen predicted it to be benign, and it was classified as likely pathogenic under ACMG criteria. The authors described GABRB1 as a new EIMFS gene that had previously been reported in other developmental and epileptic encephalopathies.","pmid":"31618474","section":"molecular_genetics"},{"id":"variant:GABRB1:p.I247T","type":"Variant","label":"GABRB1 p.I247T","gene":"GABRB1","keys":["p.I247T"],"omim_name":"GABRB1, ILE247THR"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0007359","type":"Phenotype","label":"Focal-onset seizure","hpo_id":"HP:0007359"},{"id":"finding:31618474:2","type":"Finding","label":"Burgess et al. (2019) reported a patient with a de novo GABRB1 variant who was studied at age 2 years. The patient had focal seizures beginning at 4 months, with interhemispheric seizure migration (an inclusion criterion of the cohort). The patient also had profound to severe developmental impairment and hypotonia, and did not have epileptic spasms or microcephaly.","pmid":"31618474","section":"clinical_features"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0032786","type":"Phenotype","label":"Migrating focal seizure","hpo_id":"HP:0032786"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0001263","type":"Phenotype","label":"Global developmental delay","hpo_id":"HP:0001263"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0001252","type":"Phenotype","label":"Hypotonia","hpo_id":"HP:0001252"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0011097","type":"Phenotype","label":"Epileptic spasm","hpo_id":"HP:0011097"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0000252","type":"Phenotype","label":"Microcephaly","hpo_id":"HP:0000252"},{"id":"finding:31618474:3","type":"Finding","label":"Burgess et al. (2019) found causative variants in 93 of 135 patients with EIMFS (69%), spread across 23 genes. Nine of these genes were new to EIMFS, among them GABRA1 and GABRB1. The authors emphasized the role of GABA-related mechanisms and noted that 3 patients carried variants in the known EIMFS gene GABRB3.","pmid":"31618474","section":"heterogeneity"},{"id":"gene:GABRA1","type":"Gene","label":"GABRA1"}],"edges":[{"src":"paper:23934111","rel":"reports","dst":"finding:23934111:1"},{"src":"finding:23934111:1","rel":"in_section","dst":"section:molecular_genetics"},{"src":"finding:23934111:1","rel":"mentions_gene","dst":"gene:GABRB1","pmid":"23934111"},{"src":"finding:23934111:1","rel":"reports_variant","dst":"variant:GABRB1:raw:de novo mutation in gabrb1","pmid":"23934111","zygosity":"heterozygous","origin":"de_novo","as_reported":"de novo mutation in GABRB1"},{"src":"variant:GABRB1:raw:de novo mutation in gabrb1","rel":"in_gene","dst":"gene:GABRB1"},{"src":"finding:23934111:1","rel":"has_phenotype","dst":"hpo:HP:0012469","pmid":"23934111","present":true,"text":"infantile spasms"},{"src":"finding:23934111:1","rel":"has_phenotype","dst":"hpo:HP:0002197","pmid":"23934111","present":true,"text":"Lennox-Gastaut syndrome"},{"src":"finding:23934111:1","rel":"asserts_inheritance","dst":"inh:AD","pmid":"23934111"},{"src":"paper:23934111","rel":"reports","dst":"finding:23934111:2"},{"src":"finding:23934111:2","rel":"in_section","dst":"section:clinical_features"},{"src":"finding:23934111:2","rel":"has_phenotype","dst":"hpo:HP:0002521","pmid":"23934111","present":true,"text":"hypsarrhythmia"},{"src":"finding:23934111:2","rel":"has_phenotype","dst":"hpo:HP:0010850","pmid":"23934111","present":true,"text":"generalized spike and wave discharges"},{"src":"paper:23934111","rel":"reports","dst":"finding:23934111:3"},{"src":"finding:23934111:3","rel":"in_section","dst":"section:heterogeneity"},{"src":"finding:23934111:3","rel":"mentions_gene","dst":"gene:GABRB3","pmid":"23934111"},{"src":"finding:23934111:3","rel":"mentions_gen